menu "PRISM on the Korvo-1"

    config PRISM_LCD_PCLK_MHZ
        int "RGB panel pixel clock (MHz)"
        default 18
        range 1 40
        help
            The panel's pixel clock. 18 MHz is the board-support package's own value
            (BSP_DISPLAY_PANEL_RGB_TIMING). Lower it if the picture shifts: the panel then reads
            less from PSRAM per second, at a lower refresh rate.

    config PRISM_DISPLAY_CPU_COPY
        bool "Copy the bands into the panel buffer on the CPU instead of the PPA"
        default n
        help
            The fallback if the PPA misbehaves: each band PRISM draws is copied into the panel's
            buffer by the CPU, which then cannot draw the next band meanwhile.

    choice PRISM_DISPLAY_ROTATION
        prompt "How the board is held"
        default PRISM_DISPLAY_ROTATION_0
        help
            The frame the apps draw, and where touches land in it. Turned, the frame is 480x800:
            each band is turned by the PPA (or the CPU) as it is copied into the panel's buffer.

        config PRISM_DISPLAY_ROTATION_0
            bool "Landscape (800x480)"
        config PRISM_DISPLAY_ROTATION_90
            bool "Portrait, board turned clockwise: the panel's left edge on top (480x800)"
        config PRISM_DISPLAY_ROTATION_270
            bool "Portrait, board turned counter-clockwise: the panel's right edge on top (480x800)"
    endchoice

    config PRISM_BAND_HEIGHT
        int "Rows per display band"
        default 40 if !PRISM_DISPLAY_ROTATION_0
        default 48
        range 8 96
        help
            PRISM draws the frame in bands of this many rows, two of them in internal SRAM. A
            smaller band frees internal RAM (for Bluetooth, say) at the price of more copies per
            frame. Must divide the frame's height: 480 in landscape, 800 in portrait.

    config PRISM_UI_SCALE_PERCENT
        int "UI scale of the panel apps, in percent"
        range 100 300
        default 150
        help
            100 draws the widgets at their desktop size, 150 one and a half times larger. A finger
            is bigger than a pointer: raise it if the panel is small or the buttons are hard to hit.

endmenu
